source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:1://! This module checks for conflicts between trait implementations so that Verus
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:2://! does not generate contradictory axioms for conflicting implementations.
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:3://! For example, these would be conflicting and would result in contradictory axioms:
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:4://!   impl T for bool { spec fn f() -> int { 3 } }
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:5://!   impl T for bool { spec fn f() -> int { 4 } }
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:6://! In principle, this is redundant, because rustc already checks for conflicts and will
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:7://! flag this example as conflicting.  However, it is prudent to double-check more directly
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:8://! that Verus's axioms won't be contradictory, in case Verus's VIR/AST model of Rust's traits
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:9://! ever gets out of sync with rustc's conflict checking.
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:10://! This has happened before (see https://github.com/verus-lang/verus/issues/1094 ),
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:11://! and given the complexity of Rust traits, it could happen again.
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:12://! (See, for example, "fn is_sized" in rustc_middle/src/ty/util.rs, which talks about how the result
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:13://! of is_sized "... can be an over-approximation in generic contexts, where one can have
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:14://! strange rules like `<T as Foo<'static>>::Bar: Sized` ...".)
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:15://! If Verus's model gets out of sync (because, for example, the VIR/AST model ignores lifetime
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:16://! variables), it's better to detect the conflict and halt than to generate unsound axioms.
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:17://! This may mean that Verus rejects some trait implementations that Rust accepts,
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:18://! since Verus's slightly abstracted model of the trait implementations means that
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:19://! implementations that are distinct in the original Rust code might get mapped onto overlapping
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:20://! implementations in the VIR/AST model.
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:22://! To perform the conflict checking, we reuse rustc's trait conflict checker,
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:23://! but we run rustc's trait conflict checker on code generated from Verus's VIR/AST model of the
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:24://! trait implementations rather than on the original trait implementations.
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:25://! This module generates trait declarations and trait implementations from Verus's VIR/AST model,
source/rust_verify/src/trait_conflicts.rs:26://! which are fed back to rustc as part of the code generated by trait_check.rs.
