source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:1://! Builds MIR from expressions. As a caller into this module, you
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:2://! have many options, but the first thing you have to decide is
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:3://! whether you are evaluating this expression for its *value*, its
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:4://! *location*, or as a *constant*.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:5://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:6://! Typically, you want the value: e.g., if you are doing `expr_a +
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:7://! expr_b`, you want the values of those expressions. In that case,
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:8://! you want one of the following functions. Note that if the expr has
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:9://! a type that is not `Copy`, then using any of these functions will
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:10://! "move" the value out of its current home (if any).
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:11://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:12://! - `expr_into_dest` -- writes the value into a specific location, which
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:13://!   should be uninitialized
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:14://! - `as_operand` -- evaluates the value and yields an `Operand`,
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:15://!   suitable for use as an argument to an `Rvalue`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:16://! - `as_temp` -- evaluates into a temporary; this is similar to `as_operand`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:17://!   except it always returns a fresh place, even for constants
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:18://! - `as_rvalue` -- yields an `Rvalue`, suitable for use in an assignment;
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:19://!   as of this writing, never needed outside of the `expr` module itself
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:20://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:21://! Sometimes though want the expression's *location*. An example
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:22://! would be during a match statement, or the operand of the `&`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:23://! operator. In that case, you want `as_place`. This will create a
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:24://! temporary if necessary.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:25://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:26://! Finally, if it's a constant you seek, then call
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:27://! `as_constant`. This creates a `Constant<H>`, but naturally it can
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:28://! only be used on constant expressions and hence is needed only in
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:29://! very limited contexts.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:30://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:31://! ### Implementation notes
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:32://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:33://! For any given kind of expression, there is generally one way that
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:34://! can be lowered most naturally. This is specified by the
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:35://! `Category::of` function in the `category` module. For example, a
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:36://! struct expression (or other expression that creates a new value)
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:37://! is typically easiest to write in terms of `as_rvalue` or `into`,
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:38://! whereas a reference to a field is easiest to write in terms of
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:39://! `as_place`. (The exception to this is scope and paren
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:40://! expressions, which have no category.)
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:41://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:42://! Therefore, the various functions above make use of one another in
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:43://! a descending fashion. For any given expression, you should pick
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:44://! the most suitable spot to implement it, and then just let the
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:45://! other fns cycle around. The handoff works like this:
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:46://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:47://! - `into(place)` -> fallback is to create an rvalue with `as_rvalue` and assign it to `place`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:48://! - `as_rvalue` -> fallback is to create an Operand with `as_operand` and use `Rvalue::use`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:49://! - `as_operand` -> either invokes `as_constant` or `as_temp`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:50://! - `as_constant` -> (no fallback)
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:51://! - `as_temp` -> creates a temporary and either calls `as_place` or `into`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:52://! - `as_place` -> for rvalues, falls back to `as_temp` and returns that
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:53://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:54://! As you can see, there is a cycle where `into` can (in theory) fallback to `as_temp`
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:55://! which can fallback to `into`. So if one of the `ExprKind` variants is not, in fact,
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:56://! implemented in the category where it is supposed to be, there will be a problem.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:57://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:58://! Of those fallbacks, the most interesting one is `into`, because
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:59://! it discriminates based on the category of the expression. This is
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:60://! basically the point where the "by value" operations are bridged
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/expr/mod.rs:61://! over to the "by reference" mode (`as_place`).
